const title = "Ice Buildup in Freezer? Causes & How to Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Ice or frost building up in your freezer? Usually a failed defrost system, a leaking door seal, or a clogged drain. How to tell which and when to call a tech.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why does ice keep building up in my freezer?",
    a: "In a frost-free freezer, constant ice buildup means the automatic defrost system has stopped doing its job. Normally the unit heats the evaporator coil briefly on a cycle to melt off frost before it accumulates. When the defrost heater, defrost sensor, or timer/control fails, frost is never cleared and it grows into a solid sheet of ice — usually on the back wall. The other common causes are a door seal that leaks humid room air and a door that gets left ajar or opened constantly. A manual-defrost freezer, by contrast, is supposed to frost up and simply needs periodic manual defrosting."
  },
  {
    q: "Is frost buildup in the freezer normal?",
    a: "A thin, even dusting of frost is normal, especially in humid coastal areas or if the door gets opened a lot. What isn't normal is a thick, growing sheet of ice on the back wall, ice creeping over the vents, or a slab forming on the floor of the freezer. Those point to a failed defrost system, a bad seal, or a clogged defrost drain rather than everyday frosting. If you're chipping ice more than once or twice a year on a frost-free unit, something has failed."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I get rid of ice buildup in my freezer?",
    a: "The reset is a full defrost. Empty the freezer, unplug it, and leave the door open until every bit of ice has melted — that often takes a full day, so keep towels down for the runoff. While it's thawing, clean the door gasket and check it for cracks, and make sure the door has been closing fully. Plug it back in and watch it for a week. If it cools normally and stays clear, the buildup was from a seal or door-habit issue. If the ice returns within days, the defrost system has failed and needs a tech."
  },
  {
    q: "Why is there a sheet of ice on the floor of my freezer?",
    a: "Ice forming on the floor of the freezer — often with water pooling under the fridge too — usually means the defrost drain is clogged. When the unit defrosts, the meltwater is supposed to run down a small drain tube to a pan underneath. If that tube ices over or clogs with debris, the water backs up, refreezes on the freezer floor, and eventually leaks out. Clearing the drain is sometimes a homeowner job on accessible models, but on many freezers the drain sits behind the rear panel, which is where our techs come in."
  },
  {
    q: "Will ice buildup damage my freezer?",
    a: "Left alone, yes — it makes the problem worse and costs you money. A coil buried in ice can't cool efficiently, so the compressor runs longer and harder, your energy bill climbs, and the freezer struggles to hold 0°F even as it looks packed with ice. Frost over the vents chokes airflow to the point where food starts to soften. Catching a failed defrost part early is far cheaper than letting it run a straining compressor for months. Our diagnostic identifies the failed part for $89, waived with the repair."
  }
];

