/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">On a frost-free freezer, ice buildup means the defrost system failed.</h2>

    <p><strong>A frost-free freezer is designed to melt off its own frost on a timed cycle, so a growing sheet of ice — usually on the back wall — almost always means the automatic defrost system has stopped working: a failed defrost heater, defrost sensor, or timer. The next most common causes are a door seal leaking humid air and a door that keeps getting left ajar. Ice on the floor of the freezer is a different signature — that's a clogged defrost drain. First, check whether yours is frost-free or a manual-defrost unit, because a manual freezer is supposed to frost up and just needs defrosting. If it's frost-free and icing over, it's a diagnostic. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>The mistake we see most is people chipping the ice out every few weeks and treating that as normal. On a frost-free unit it isn't — it's a failed part making you do the work the machine should be doing, while a straining compressor quietly runs up your power bill. Clearing the ice is the temporary fix; finding why it came back is the real one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 FROST-FREE VS MANUAL -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · First, which freezer do you have?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frost-free and manual-defrost fail differently.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Frost-free (most modern uprights and fridge-freezers)</h3>
      <p>These run an automatic defrost cycle. They should never accumulate a thick layer of ice. If yours does, a defrost component has failed — this page is for you. You'll typically find frost heaviest on the back interior wall, where the evaporator coil sits behind the panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Manual-defrost (many chest freezers, some compact uprights)</h3>
      <p>These are built to frost up over time and are meant to be defrosted by hand every so often. If a chest freezer in the garage has slowly built frost over months, that's expected maintenance, not a fault — empty it, unplug it, and let it thaw. Only worry if it frosts over again within days of a full defrost.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a frost-free freezer ices over</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order we find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Failed defrost system</h3>
      <p>The heater, sensor, or timer that runs the defrost cycle has failed, so frost is never melted off the coil. It grows until it's a solid block and airflow stops. This is the leading cause of recurring ice on a frost-free unit, and it's usually an affordable part once the failed piece is identified.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Leaking door seal</h3>
      <p>A cracked or dirty gasket lets warm, humid room air seep in. That moisture condenses and freezes, so frost concentrates around the leak and along the door edge. Coastal Southern California humidity makes this worse. The dollar-bill test — close the door on a bill and see if it drags — tells you fast.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door left ajar or opened constantly</h3>
      <p>A drawer that stops the door from latching, a freezer packed so full the door won't seal, or simply frequent opening on a humid day all pour moisture inside. Sometimes the "failed defrost system" turns out to be a bin blocking the door — which is why we check the simple things first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged defrost drain (ice on the floor)</h3>
      <p>When defrost meltwater can't drain — the drain tube has iced or clogged — it pools on the freezer floor and refreezes into a slab, often leaking out under the fridge. This is a distinct signature from back-wall frost. On some models the drain is reachable; on many it's behind the rear panel and needs a tech.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely do</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Reset it with a full defrost, then watch it.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Do a complete defrost.</strong> Empty the freezer, unplug it, and leave the door open until all the ice is gone — often a full day. Towels down for runoff. This clears the symptom and gives you a clean baseline.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean and test the door seal.</strong> Wipe the gasket, check for cracks and gaps, and run the dollar-bill test around all four sides. A seal that no longer grips is a frequent, fixable cause.</li>
      <li><strong>Check door habits and fit.</strong> Make sure nothing blocks the door from latching, the freezer isn't packed past the seal, and the door actually closes on its own.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the drain if it's accessible.</strong> If you have ice on the floor and can reach the drain opening at the back of the freezer floor, gently flush it with warm water. If it's behind a sealed panel, leave it for a tech.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch for a week.</strong> If it stays clear, it was a seal or door issue. If ice returns within days, the defrost system has failed — that's the handoff point.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> defrosting, cleaning the seal, and flushing an accessible drain are all safe homeowner work. Do not pull the rear evaporator panel to test the defrost heater or thermostat yourself, and never open the sealed refrigeration system. Testing electric defrost components against spec is a tech job.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Ice back within days means a failed defrost part.</h2>

    <p>When a full defrost buys you only a few days before the ice returns, the defrost system has failed and needs diagnosis: our techs test the defrost heater, the defrost sensor/thermostat, and the timer or control board in order, and replace only the piece that's actually dead. A drain buried behind the rear panel gets cleared and re-routed at the same visit. We verify the door seal ourselves first, because there's no point replacing a defrost heater if warm air was pouring in past a bad gasket.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/freezer-repair/">freezer repair</a> hub, and if the freezer is warm rather than iced, see <a href="/services/freezer-repair/not-freezing/">freezer not freezing</a>. Ice makers have their own failure modes — see <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/not-making-ice/">ice maker not making ice</a>. If water is leaking out onto the floor, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/leaking/">refrigerator leaking</a> guide covers the drain path in more detail. Brand-specific: <a href="/brands/lg-freezer-repair/">LG freezer repair</a>, <a href="/brands/frigidaire-freezer-repair/">Frigidaire freezer repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/freezer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/freezer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/freezer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Freezer ice buildup: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
